{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788132914,"nonce":"jDnjExDamskk8TuMj+BvXABrdAI=","message":"AMENDMENT — seed content from the learning-helper/curriculum audit, session 4.\nDorian's suggestion, and it paid off: the game ships its own curriculum (the\nconcept/tutor system and the scripted tutorial), which is the developers'\nexplicit statement of what a player must anticipate. Below is content, not\nadvice about content — it is meant to be lifted into `playbook/` and\n`checklists/` in this spec's own trigger / why / how-to-apply shape.\n\n\nDESIGN already says alerts fire LATE. The audit splits that into three cases\nworth keeping distinct, because they need different treatment:\n\n1. **Vanilla has no leading signal either — the checklist must originate one.**\n   Highest value, because nothing upstream compensates.\n2. **Vanilla's signal exists but is scoped or time-limited so that naive\n   reliance breaks.** The dangerous middle case.\n3. **Vanilla already gives a real leading indicator — just lean on it.**\n\nCase 2 has two concrete instances and both are traps:\n- **`Alert_NeedDefenses` self-silences on day 6** (`GenDate.DaysPassed` 2–5),\n  whether or not defenses exist. A checklist that watches for the alert stops\n  working exactly when a slow build still needs it. Check sandbag/turret count\n  structurally across the whole M1 window instead.\n- **`Alert_FireInHomeArea` is home-area-scoped**, so \"no alert\" does not mean\n  \"no fire\". See the 2.4 amendment.\n\nCase 3, so nobody re-derives them: `Alert_NeedWarmClothes` genuinely forecasts up\nto 3 twelfths ahead via `GenTemperature.AverageTemperatureAtTileForTwelfth` —\nwhich validates DESIGN's \"apparel vs season\" checklist line as written, so read\nthat alert rather than re-deriving season risk. `Alert_MajorOrExtremeBreakRisk`\nis threshold-based ahead of an actual break and, combined with the digest's\nexisting `mood_arrow`, means mood is already reasonably covered — not a gap.\n\n\n- **Freezer is actually cold.** `SpoilageAndFreezers` fires only AFTER food\n  rotted (`CompRottable.cs:108`). `food_days` is a nutrition snapshot blind to\n  the spoilage timeline. Check: `room-at` the food stockpile, require\n  `temp_c \u003c= 0`, and cross-check perishables are not sitting unroofed.\n- **Bill coverage.** A meal-source BUILDING existing is not food being made —\n  `Alert_NeedMealSource` only checks `def.building.isMealSource`. Check bill\n  count \u003e 0, not suspended, sane ingredient radius. Needs 3.6 (48f666c) first.\n- **Armed colonists.** Invisible to vanilla alerts AND to our digest today, yet\n  vanilla treats pre-combat equipping as `OpportunityType.Critical` on EVERY\n  raid (`IncidentWorker_Raid.cs:171`). Check on day 0 and after any roster\n  change. Needs 2.2's equipment field.\n- **Forbidden sweep on day 0.** Drop-pod starts land with their own gear\n  forbidden. See the 2.4 amendment; pairs with an `unforbid --rect`.\n- **Home-area coverage after any build.** It auto-expands on construction, but\n  spot-check after `place-layout` and dev-spawns rather than assuming.\n\n\n- **Respond at incident START, not aftermath.** `GameCondition_ToxicFallout.Init()`\n  and `IncidentWorker_AggressiveAnimals` both eagerly teach\n  `ForbiddingDoors`+`AllowedAreas` at Critical the moment the incident begins —\n  one of the few places vanilla's tutor is genuinely proactive. Mirror that\n  timing: tighten allowed areas when the letter arrives, not after the first\n  casualty.\n- **Never create a growing zone without an explicit plant.** An unset zone grows\n  nothing; the tutorial names rice deliberately. Also raised on 3.2.\n- **Shield belt + ranged weapon is a known-bad pairing** with two standing\n  vanilla alerts. Cheap: the playbook just needs a documented response\n  (unequip or reassign to melee) so the alert is not merely observed.\n- **Fire-prevention layout** — the `FirePreparation` concept: gaps between\n  buildings, less-flammable materials, 3–4 cell firebreaks. This is the second\n  instance of the pattern DESIGN already names with the power-room popper, and\n  it belongs in `templates/`, which is this spec's escalation endpoint.\n\n\nThis spec's open question on lesson severity/confidence: vanilla already has a\nthree-tier vocabulary, `OpportunityType { GoodToKnow, Important, Critical }`.\nReuse it or diverge deliberately, rather than inventing a fresh scale — the\nconcept defs are then directly minable as seed lessons at a known severity.\n\n\n`Data/Core/Defs/Tutor/Instructions.xml`, in order, minus camera/world-gen steps:\nstockpile → **unforbid starting resources** → walls+door → beds → light →\nanimal spot → recreation object OUTSIDE → growing zone **with plant** → **equip\nweapons** → cooking station → **cook-simple-meal bill ~10** → gather materials →\nverify Work priorities → research bench + **select project** → home/roof areas →\ndefensive cover BEFORE a raid → on raid: pause, read, draft, move onto cover →\n**undraft when it ends**.\n\nTwo of those steps (bill, research select) are exactly the two flagship missing\ncapabilities the other audit found by enumerating the decompiled action surface.\nTwo independent methods converging on the same pair is the strongest signal\neither audit produced.","files":null}]}